One of TiddlyWiki's most powerful capabilities is its non-linear, bidirectional linking system. Slipstream enhances this architecture with a dedicated **Right Context Inspector** that stays dynamically synchronized with whatever note you are actively reading.

---

! The Power of Bidirectional Links

When you write notes in Slipstream, you link concepts together using double square brackets: `[[Note Name]]`.

Unlike traditional static web pages or file-tree editors, links in TiddlyWiki are two-way connections:
1. **Outbound links**: Direct paths to other notes in your wiki.
2. **Inbound backlinks**: References from other notes pointing back to this one.

---

! Inspecting Connections

Look at the **Right Context Inspector** panel on the right side of your desktop screen:

* **Backlinks Tab**: Shows every note in your wiki that links here! For example, you should see [[Welcome to Slipstream]] and [[Knowledge Base]] listed as backlinks right now.
* **Outline Tab**: Displays the heading hierarchy (`!`, `!!`, `!!!`) of this active note.
* **Fields Tab**: Lets you view, edit, add, and remove custom fields directly on the active note.

> [!TIP]
> **Mobile Viewing**: On mobile screens (under `1024px`), access the inspector anytime by tapping the **Context** (`🔗`) button in the bottom navigation bar.

---

! Workflows You Can Build

Because Slipstream relies purely on standard TiddlyWiki tagging and linking, you can build any personal workflow without locking yourself into proprietary plugins:

# **Zettelkasten / Digital Garden**: Interlink atomic ideas and use the inspector's backlinks to discover unexpected connections.
# **Topic Indexes & MOCs (Maps of Content)**: Use [[Knowledge Base]] as an entry point to cluster related subjects.
# **Project Notes & Reference Sheets**: Track documentation, meetings, and ideas with inline property pills.

